{
  "title_card": "Why Is The Sky Blue",
  "voice": "am_michael",
  "speed": 1.12,
  "lines": [
    {"scene": 0, "text": "You're looking up on a perfectly clear afternoon, and the whole sky is glowing blue."},
    {"scene": 0, "text": "But sunlight isn't blue at all — it's white."},
    {"scene": 1, "text": "That white light is every color mixed together, from red to violet."},
    {"scene": 1, "text": "So where did all the other colors go?"},
    {"scene": 1, "text": "Physicists call this Rayleigh scattering."},
    {"scene": 2, "text": "Here's the catch: air is packed with tiny nitrogen and oxygen molecules, far smaller than a wave of light."},
    {"scene": 3, "text": "When sunlight hits them, short blue waves get bounced around nearly six times more than long red waves."},
    {"scene": 4, "text": "So blue light ricochets across the whole sky and reaches your eyes from every direction."},
    {"scene": 4, "text": "Red and yellow mostly pass straight through."},
    {"scene": 5, "text": "At sunset, light crosses so much air that the blue gets scattered away — leaving the reds behind."},
    {"scene": 5, "text": "And tomorrow afternoon, when you're looking up"}
  ],
